## Similarities between ProcessWire and Wordpress

- PHP
- Apache
- MySQL
- Open Source

## ProcessWire

```github
processwire/processwire | Un CMS libre de código abierto con una API de desarrollo
```

- [https://processwire.com/](https://processwire.com/)

### Pros

- Released in 2010. In development since 2003. (Dictator CMS).
- Highly secure. CVE-ID: 0
  <https://cve.mitre.org/cgi-bin/cvekey.cgi?keyword=processwire>
- Highly customizable.
- Highly scalable. Supporting millions of pages without problems.
- Built-in Roles system.
- Multi-language included. Easy to translate.
- Easy to apply CMS updates.
- API easy to learn and use. It is pure PHP.
- Friendly, active community with direct communication with the CMS
  creator.
- Allows multiple instances, making it easy to design lightweight and
  robust software.
- Easy to adapt an HTML design into a self-managed one.
- Used in several sites with high amounts of content.
- Module system to expand functionalities.
- Weekly CMS improvements, backwards compatible with previous
  versions (new versions cause no conflicts between modules and
  systems with previous versions).
- The system has a structure that adapts to the way a person or team
  works.

### Cons

- Less known than Wordpress.
- Requires more technical knowledge for development. (Although the
  end user needs nothing special since the system can be adapted to
  their needs). For the same reason some areas may take a bit more
  time, but they will be more personalized and focused.
- Fewer plugins and modules. (Many Wordpress plugins are not needed
  with ProcessWire since it integrates them or they are easy to
  implement directly).
- Fewer themes (designs) already adapted to ProcessWire. (Adapting
  an HTML design is very simple and allows a more customized system
  than an already elaborated and generic Wordpress theme).

## Wordpress

```github
WordPress/WordPress | El gestor de contenidos más usado del web
```

- [https://wordpress.org](https://wordpress.org)

### Pros

- Widely known by the industry since 2003. Greater amount of
  technical support and documentation.
- Large amount of generic themes.
- Large amount of plugins (free and paid).
- Speed for prototyping a site.

### Cons

- Highly insecure. Updates can corrupt a site or expose it to
  hacking for not being monitored and constant. Using plugins can
  reduce security and performance by having no control over their
  content and compatibility problems between them. CVE-ID: 1024
  <https://cve.mitre.org/cgi-bin/cvekey.cgi?keyword=wordpress>
- Slowness. Wordpress systems are known for consuming large amounts
  of resources and running slower than other systems.
- Difficulty creating custom systems. Being a Blog system, many
  patchy and inelegant solutions are required to create a unique
  experience.
- Hard to understand API. The functions to access the information
  are unintuitive, increasing development times.
- Defined and inflexible theme structure. The person or development
  team must adapt to Wordpress.
